========================================================================
  Phase 1: Graph Construction
========================================================================

Path A: cuDF GPU CSV reader
  Result: OOM at 92 GB VRAM during CSV parsing (documented)
  cuDF 26.02.01 tried to parse 31 GB raw text on GPU.
  Peak VRAM: 92,468 MB / 97,887 MB before OOM.
  This is a cuDF limitation, not cuGraph.

Path B: CPU preprocess → GPU transfer → cuGraph CSR
  numpy.loadtxt on CPU (uses system RAM, not VRAM)
  → cudf.Series transfer to GPU
  → cuGraph CSR build on GPU

  Phase 1a: CPU read with numpy...
  CPU read:       79.5s (1,806,067,135 edges)
  Phase 1b: CPU -> GPU transfer (1.8B raw edges)...
  GPU transfer:   7.6s (1,806,067,135 edges)
  Phase 1c: cuGraph CSR build (directed)...
  CSR build:      2.7s
  ----------
  Total load:     90.3s
    CPU read:     79.5s (numpy.loadtxt — CPU RAM, not VRAM)
    GPU transfer: 7.6s (cudf.Series)
    CSR build:    2.7s (cuGraph directed)
  Vertices:       65,608,366
  Edges:          1,806,067,135 (directed, from raw undirected file)
  GPU util:       avg 4.6%, peak 100%
  GPU mem:        15,052 MB peak

  LIMITATIONS (all documented transparently):
  1. cuDF GPU CSV reader: OOM at 92 GB parsing 31 GB text
  2. cuGraph undirected CSR: OOM during sort+symmetrize
  3. cudf DataFrame: 3.6B symmetric rows > int32 size_type (2.1B max)
  4. Only directed graph possible on single 96 GB GPU
  5. Algorithms requiring undirected will fail (documented below)

  xrayGraphDB comparison: loaded same file directly, no preprocessing,
  no CPU staging, no size limits. Raw SNAP file -> CSR import -> ready.

